pySMESH: make StdMeshers_Adaptive1D hold its deflection (SMESH V9_16_0).

Defect: the Adaptive hypothesis documents three rules (doc/gui/input/1d_meshing_hypo.rst):
the segment length "is limited by Min. Size and Max Size", adjacent segments "can't differ
more than twice", and the "Deflection parameter gives maximal distance of a segment from a
curved edge". The algorithm can break the third rule.

Symptom: on a curved EDGE next to a curved FACE, segments are longer than the deflection
allows. Radius-1.5 rims of a cylinder, deflection 0.01: 28 segments, the longest 0.352196,
sagitta 0.0103727 = 1.037 x the deflection (1.093 x at deflection 0.003). The same on an
ellipse 2 x 1.5 (1.042 x) and on radius-4 rims at deflection 0.02 (1.025 x). The lone circle
(no FACE) gives 28 equal chords of 0.335893, sagitta 0.00943, within the deflection.

Root cause: the deflection only seeds the size field. SegSizeTree::SetSize(p1, p2) stores
the size of a deflection chord at its two ends and its middle; the octree leaves around them
keep the size that SetSize propagates, size + boxSize * grading (0.3359 + 0.7 * 0.2532 =
0.5131 on the rim above). The loop that limits the size by the distance to the FACEs
(AdaptiveAlgo::Compute) inserts probe points at the middles of probe intervals with the tree
size there; on the rim 6 of 44 probes carry 0.5131 instead of 0.3359. makeSegments()
interpolates the probe sizes, places the nodes on the integral of 1 / size and rounds the
count; around those probes the segments grow to 0.352. No segment is checked against the
deflection. The code is unchanged since the hypothesis was added (SMESH 7b33bc39f,
2013-11-11, "22355: EDF SMESH: New 1D hypothesis Adaptive"); upstream master at 83f8bf482
(2026-09-14) has the same code.

Sagitta of a segment, as used here: the largest distance from the EDGE curve, between the
segment's two end parameters, to the chord (16 samples, then a golden-section search next
to the largest one).

Change, in StdMeshers_Adaptive1D.cxx only:
* makeSegments(): after the parameters of an EDGE are computed, call holdDeflection().
* AdaptiveAlgo::holdDeflection() (new): if every segment meets the deflection, the
  parameters stay as they are, bit for bit. Otherwise, over each segment that misses it
  where the size field is above its floor (the min size), lower the probe sizes to
  0.9 x min( L sqrt( deflection / sagitta ), field size ); sample the field on a parameter
  grid (buildGrid: at least 10 grid steps per segment) and grade it (a size grows by at most
  0.5 x the distance, so adjacent segments differ by at most e^0.5 = 1.65); take the count
  ceil( integral of 1 / size ), kept within the counts that min size and max size allow; scale
  the field so that its integral is that count, kept within [floor, max size]; place the
  nodes (distribute). Repeat until every segment meets the deflection or lies where the field
  is at the floor. If an arc of the floor size gives a chord shorter than the min size, the
  floor is raised by the shortfall, so that no chord is shorter than the min size.
* New helpers: segmentSagitta(), EdgeGrid, probeFieldMax(), lowerProbeSize(), buildGrid(),
  distribute().

Order of the rules when they conflict: min size and max size are hard bounds. The deflection
holds unless meeting it would need a segment shorter than the min size; there the min size
wins: those segments lie where the field is at the floor, at least min size long. Where the
whole EDGE is in that case, the count is the largest whose segments are not below min size;
on a closed circle of radius R the chords are then equal, 2 R sin(pi / n) with
n = floor(pi / asin(min size / 2 R)), and equal to min size only if that ratio is whole.
The size tree's own floor, 2e-6 x the shape's box, stays the smallest size.

Not changed: an EDGE whose segments all meet the deflection keeps the segments of
makeSegments(), including where they break the min size or the factor 2 (seen upstream on
B-spline and cone EDGEs). The size tree, the other EDGEs and Evaluate() are untouched. If the
deflection is still missed after 200 refinements, the EDGE fails with a compute error.

